    ```+solution
    function makeStream(name, trackNames) {
      const stream = { name: name };

      stream.getTracks = function () {
        return trackNames;
      };

      return stream;
    }

    const remoteVideo = { srcObject: null };

    const peerConnection = { added: [], listeners: {} };

    peerConnection.addTrack = function (track, stream) {
      peerConnection.added.push(track);
    };

    peerConnection.addEventListener = function (name, callback) {
      peerConnection.listeners[name] = callback;
    };

    const localStream = makeStream('mine', ['video', 'audio']);

    peerConnection.addEventListener('track', (trackEvent) => {
      remoteVideo.srcObject = trackEvent.streams[0];
    });

    for (const track of localStream.getTracks()) {
      peerConnection.addTrack(track, localStream);
    }

    // Media starts arriving from the other side.
    peerConnection.listeners.track({ streams: [makeStream('theirs', ['video', 'audio'])] });

    console.log(peerConnection.added, remoteVideo.srcObject);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(peerConnection.added).should('equal', ['video', 'audio']).run('Loop over `localStream.getTracks()` and add each track with `peerConnection.addTrack(track, localStream)`', 'Sent both tracks');
    wrap(remoteVideo.srcObject && remoteVideo.srcObject.name).should('equal', 'theirs').run('In the `track` listener, set `remoteVideo.srcObject` to `trackEvent.streams[0]`', 'Displayed the remote stream');
    wrap(/addEventListener\s*\(\s*['"]track['"]/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Listen for the `track` event to know when media starts arriving', 'Listened for arriving media');
    ```

3. The remote box stays black

    ```{mcq}
    Your own video shows fine and the consoles show the handshake completing, but the remote box stays black in both windows. `createPeerConnection` looks like this. Which piece is missing?

    ~~~js
    async function createPeerConnection() {
      peerConnection = new RTCPeerConnection(configuration);

      peerConnection.addEventListener('icecandidate', (iceEvent) => {
        if (iceEvent.candidate) {
          ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'candidate', candidate: iceEvent.candidate }));
        }
      });
    }
    ~~~

    correct: Correct! A connection carries only what you attach to it. `addTrack` is what sends your media, and the `track` listener is what displays theirs; with neither, the handshake still completes and the connection carries nothing.
    wrong: The handshake completed, so the signaling and the descriptions are fine. What puts media ON a finished connection, and what displays media coming off it?
    [x] The media wiring: no `addTrack` calls and no `track` listener
    [ ] The `muted` attribute is missing from the remote video element
    [ ] The STUN server is refusing to carry the video
    [ ] `JSON.stringify` cannot handle video data
    ```

4. Muting the right video

    ```{mcq}
    In the page, the local video element has `muted` and the remote one does not. Why that way around?

    ~~~html
    <video id="local-video" autoplay playsinline muted></video>
    <video id="remote-video" autoplay playsinline></video>
    ~~~

    correct: Correct! Muting your own video stops your microphone from playing through your speakers and back into your microphone as feedback. The remote video must stay unmuted, because hearing the other person is the point of the call.
    wrong: Think about where each element's audio comes from, and what happens when a microphone can hear its own speakers.
    [x] Muting your own video prevents a feedback loop; the remote audio is the call itself
    [ ] `muted` stops your camera from recording until the call starts
    [ ] It hides the local video element until a stream arrives
    [ ] The browser requires the first video element on a page to be muted
    ```

